A painting application applies interchangeable colour‑adjustment algorithms to an image. The class that holds the image data and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `HueShift` and `ContrastBoost` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void applyAdjustment(Image img); }
public class HueShift implements SortStrategy { /* ... */ }
public class ContrastBoost implements SortStrategy { /* ... */ }
public class Context { private Image img; private SortStrategy adjustment; /* ... */ }
public class Policy { private Context context; public void configure() { /* ... */ } }
```

A reviewer rejects the following class:

```java
public class BrushService {
    private final PaintProvider provider = new PaintProvider("paint.internal", 25);
    public void applyBrush(Brush brush) { /* ... */ provider.supply(brush.getColour()); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `BrushService` names a concrete low-level class instead of an abstraction such as a `PaintProvider` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
